I. THE PARADIGM SHIFT THAT CREATES THIS PROFESSION

The history of labor transitions has a grammar. When the loom was automated, weavers became machine operators. When the tractor arrived, farmhands became mechanics. When the spreadsheet replaced the bookkeeper, bookkeepers became analysts. Each transition followed the same structural logic: a tool became more capable, humans moved up a level of abstraction, and the workforce reorganized around the new division of labor.

That grammar stops working on the Moon.

The World Economic Forum projects AI will create 97 million new jobs globally, with AI roles commanding a median salary of $160,056 in 2024. Stanford's SALT Lab documents that 70 million US workers are already navigating the biggest workplace transition in modern history — and that the central unsolved problem is mapping what workers want AI to automate versus what they need to retain. A manager who once managed ten human agents now manages one agent plus an AI system handling the routine work of the other nine. [Gaper] These projections describe an Earth economy with unemployment absorption mechanisms, retraining programs, geographic labor mobility, and a market that corrects mismatch between human capability and economic demand.

None of those mechanisms exist on the Moon.

A lunar colony operating at the 2035–2050 horizon will house 50 to 200 humans alongside thousands of AI agents managing atmospheric control, energy distribution, food production, structural monitoring, and logistical coordination — and hundreds of semi-autonomous robots executing physical tasks across the habitat and surrounding operational zones. The humans in this colony cannot be redeployed to a different city if their role is automated. They cannot be retrained by an external institution. They cannot leave the workforce.

What they can do — what the research on confined, isolated environments shows they will do, reliably — is deteriorate psychologically when their work loses meaning, degrade operationally when their skills atrophy without use, and destabilize socially when authority over the systems that sustain their lives becomes unclear.

The AI Workforce Designer is the profession responsible for ensuring none of these happen — by designing, before the first colonist arrives, the labor topology of the colony: what AI handles autonomously, what AI recommends and humans decide, what humans execute regardless of AI capability, and how those divisions evolve as the colony's mission phases change over years and decades.

This is not a human resources function. It is a systems architecture function. And it may be the most consequential architectural decision the colony makes.

