II. WHAT THIS PROFESSION ACTUALLY DOES

The AI Workforce Designer designs the division of cognitive, operational, and executive labor between human colonists and AI agent systems in a closed, isolated environment where that division cannot be revised by market forces, external hiring, or geographic exit.

This definition contains four elements that each demand separate engineering treatment.

Cognitive, operational, and executive labor. Not "humans do this, AI does that." The division operates at three distinct levels simultaneously. Cognitive labor is the thinking, judging, and sense-making that produces decisions. Operational labor is the execution of those decisions — physical or computational. Executive labor is the authorization of action under uncertainty: the authority to proceed when no algorithm can certify the outcome is safe. All three levels must be explicitly designed. None can be left to emerge organically, because in an isolated colony, the organic emergence of labor division will follow the path of least resistance — maximum delegation to AI — until a critical skill or authority structure has been lost without anyone noticing.

AI agent systems, not tools. The distinction matters because the psychological relationship between a human and an agent is categorically different from the relationship between a human and a tool. A tool extends human capability. An agent competes for cognitive territory. An AI system that pursues objectives, makes decisions mid-task, adapts to changing conditions, and maintains operational continuity without continuous human supervision is an agent. The Cohabitation Engineer profession establishes the physical design requirements of sharing space with robotic agents. The AI Workforce Designer establishes the organizational design requirements of sharing authority with cognitive agents.

A closed, isolated environment. The closure is what makes the design problem non-trivial. On Earth, a poorly designed human-AI labor division corrects itself through attrition, retraining, and market feedback. In a colony sealed 384,000 kilometers from the nearest external correction mechanism, a poorly designed division persists until it produces a failure. The designer cannot wait for market feedback. The design must be right before the colony is inhabited.

Where revision by market forces is not available. This is the constraint that eliminates every workforce design framework developed in the past century of management science. All existing theory assumes the possibility of adjustment: hire more people with skill X, outsource function Y, dissolve team Z. On the Moon, the workforce is fixed at launch. Every person who will ever be in that colony is either already there or known by name before the mission begins. The AI Workforce Designer designs for that roster, for that mission duration, with no assumption that external adjustment is possible.

