IV. FAILURE MODE 1 — SKILL ATROPHY AND THE COMPETENCY FLOOR PROTOCOL

Aviation has a name for what happens when pilots delegate too much to autopilot systems: glass cockpit syndrome. The pilot can manage the aircraft's computers fluently. They can no longer fly the aircraft manually with competence. The skill that the automation was designed to augment has been replaced by the skill of managing the automation — and when the automation fails, the pilot's hands do not remember what to do.

On a lunar colony, this failure mode scales to life-critical infrastructure.

An AI system managing atmospheric composition is more reliable than any human operator across 99.97% of operating conditions. Over months of operation, the human assigned to atmospheric oversight spends their time reviewing AI reports, approving AI recommendations, and escalating AI-flagged anomalies. They do not troubleshoot atmospheric systems manually. They do not diagnose sensor drift. They do not recalibrate CO2 scrubbers by hand. The AI handles all of it.

Eighteen months in, the AI fails in a way its training data did not include — a failure mode the colony's engineers designed around but the AI's model never encountered. The human operator is called to take manual control of atmospheric management for a system they have reviewed daily but not operated manually since pre-launch training, eighteen months ago. Under pressure, in a sealed environment, with the lives of fifty people depending on their next decision, they discover that daily familiarity with an AI's outputs is not the same as operational mastery of the underlying system.

This is not a hypothetical. It is the documented trajectory of automation dependency in every safety-critical domain that has undergone deep AI integration: aviation, nuclear operations, intensive care medicine. The pattern is consistent enough to carry a name: automation-induced complacency, and its more severe form, automation-induced skill atrophy.

The architectural solution is the Competency Floor Protocol — a mandatory operational specification, not a training specification.

The distinction is critical. A training specification would require colonists to periodically practice manual operation of systems they normally allow AI to manage. The Competency Floor Protocol is more demanding: it specifies that for every life-critical system in the colony, human manual operation is a required component of regular operations — not a contingency reserved for AI failure.

The protocol has four design principles.

Mandatory manual cycles. Every life-critical system has a defined schedule on which humans operate it manually, without AI assistance, for a specified duration. Not as drill. As actual colony operation. The atmospheric systems run under human manual control for six hours every two weeks. The power distribution system runs under human management for four hours every week. The structural monitoring system is manually interpreted and reported by a human analyst without AI augmentation for one full day each month. These cycles are non-negotiable: not shortened when the colony is busy, not waived when the AI is performing well, not converted to simulation when the manual operation feels unnecessary.

Hardware-Enforced Analog Air-Gap. Humans under high cognitive load naturally seek the path of least resistance. A colonist facing a mandatory six-hour atmospheric manual cycle, knowing that an AI system could handle the task with lower effort and higher precision, will be tempted — consciously or not — to script, macro, or otherwise automate their manual responsibilities using unauthorized local code. The habitat's physical infrastructure must foreclose this possibility entirely. During mandatory manual cycles on life-critical systems, the digital telemetry lines connecting the human's control interface to the AI clusters are physically decoupled via electro-mechanical relays. The operator interfaces with an isolated, deterministic, analog control loop. The AI cannot assist. The human cannot cheat. The competency measured is genuine.

The Competency Floor is defined before AI deployment. For every life-critical system, before any AI agent is authorized to manage it, the AI Workforce Designer specifies the minimum competency level that human operators must maintain throughout the system's operational life. This specification becomes the standard against which manual operation cycles are evaluated. If a colonist's manual performance falls below the competency floor during a mandatory cycle, the protocol requires remediation before the AI resumes management of that system.

The Competency Floor is transparent to the crew. Every colonist knows which systems have mandatory manual cycles, when they occur, and what the standard requires. The protocol is not administered as an evaluation or a test — it is administered as a shift responsibility. The colonist operating the atmospheric system manually for six hours this week is not being assessed. They are doing their job. The AI is the standby.

The Competency Floor Protocol does something counterintuitive to productivity-focused workforce design: it deliberately accepts some operational efficiency loss in exchange for a preserved human capability that has no monetary value until the day the AI fails. On Earth, this tradeoff would be evaluated economically and probably rejected. On the Moon, it is the difference between a colony that survives its first major AI failure and one that does not.

