X. THE THREE DESIGN LAYERS — WORKFORCE EDITION

These five failure modes integrate into three design layers as engineering constraints, not afterthoughts.

Layer 1 — Structural Workforce Architecture

(Designed and locked before the colony roster is finalized)

Layer 1 carries five mandatory specifications before the colony is inhabited.

Decision Topology Specification: Every decision class that can arise during colony operations classified, authority level assigned, time limits specified for human response at Level 2, and the Latency-Bounded Safe-State Invariant defined per decision class — the specific conservative rollback protocol triggered when a human fails to act within the hard-coded time bound. This document is co-signed by the mission commander, the lead AI systems engineer, and the AI Workforce Designer. It carries constitutional status in the colony's operational governance.

Competency Floor Registry: Every life-critical system in the colony has a defined human competency floor, a mandatory manual operation cycle schedule, a hardware-enforced analog air-gap specification ensuring digital telemetry lines to AI clusters are physically decoupled during manual cycles, and a remediation protocol for colonists whose manual performance falls below the floor. This registry is established before any AI agent is authorized to manage a life-critical system.

Colony Health Index Design: The three-component measurement system is specified with passive telemetry data collection mechanisms built into the colony's infrastructure — structural voice analysis, spatial proximity mapping, sleep architecture monitoring, and collective vocal synchrony sensors integrated into the habitat environmental monitoring fabric. No self-reported psychological instruments are deployed. The public display system is installed as habitat infrastructure — not as an optional management tool.

Adaptive Role Architecture: Every human role in the initial colony workforce has a complete four-element specification: primary function, transition specification (including psychological preparation for physical labor grief), development pathway, and legacy function (structured to provide sanctioned channels for competencies that would otherwise drive shadow automation). No role enters the colony roster without all four elements defined.

Algorithmic Predictability Index Configuration: The shadow model architecture, phase-dependent threshold parameters (\tau and \Delta t for construction and operations phases), and contextual modifier function coefficients (\alpha, \beta) are specified and validated before colony deployment. The API audit loop is integrated into the habitat's core infrastructure as a Layer 1 utility.

Layer 2 — Behavioral Protocol Design

(Operational specifications for human-AI interaction)

Layer 2 carries four mandatory behavioral specifications for every AI agent system in the colony.

Authority Escalation Protocol: The protocol by which a human exercise of authority at any level of the Decision Topology overrides AI system behavior immediately, completely, and without requiring the AI system's confirmation. The human says stop; the AI stops. This is not a software permission system. It is an architectural invariant.

Latency-Bounded Safe-State Trigger: The hard-coded protocol that executes when a Level 2 recommendation times out under anomalous conditions and the human operator fails to confirm, modify, or reject within the specified time bound. The system does not escalate to a paralyzed human. It triggers the physics-defined conservative rollback protocol — stripping the affected system to its most baseline, predictable configuration. This trigger is an immutable architectural invariant.

Purpose Verification Cycle: A continuous API-driven audit supplemented by quarterly human review in which every colonist participates in an assessment of whether their role's irreducibility specification remains valid. Where the API has triggered a Role Architecture Review, or where human review detects erosion not yet captured by the API, the AI Workforce Designer revises the role's irreducibility specification before the next quarter begins. The purpose of the cycle is maintenance, not evaluation.

Competency Floor Cycle Management: The operational protocol for mandatory manual operation cycles — scheduling, hardware air-gap activation, performance assessment, remediation triggers, and documentation — administered as shift responsibility, not as examination.

Layer 3 — Colony Infrastructure

(Built into the physical and operational environment from day one)

Layer 3 carries three infrastructure elements that belong to the colony the way power distribution belongs to the colony — as utilities, not optional features.

Colony Health Index Display: A real-time, public display of all three components of the Colony Health Index, present in every common area of the habitat. Not a management dashboard visible only to leadership. A public instrument, as visible as the atmospheric status board.

Purpose Architecture Registry: A living document, accessible to every colonist, specifying the irreducibility of every human role in the colony. Not a human resources document. A philosophical and operational document that answers the question "why are humans here?" with the specificity the question deserves — and that is updated every time a role transitions across mission phases or the API triggers a review.

API Audit Infrastructure: The shadow model computation environment, sensor integration fabric for telemetry ingestion, and the automated escalation pipeline for Role Architecture Reviews. This infrastructure runs on isolated computational resources to prevent interference with operational AI systems.

