AI Systems Architecture — not for the purpose of building AI systems, but for understanding their failure modes, their capability trajectories, and the specific ways in which AI automation erodes human skill when the skill-erosion dynamics are not explicitly countered in system design. The practitioner must understand what AI agents are doing when they do it, at a level sufficient to specify the boundaries of their authority with precision. Includes training on behavioral cloning models and information-theoretic predictability metrics that form the foundation of the Algorithmic Predictability Index.

Human Factors in Isolated, Confined Environments — the full ICE research corpus: MARS-500, Antarctic overwinter studies, submarine deployment data, HI-SEAS and FMARS analog research. The specific literature on meaning, motivation, and identity under isolation. The clinical literature on learned helplessness, automation-induced complacency, and the psychological determinants of operational resilience in groups with no exit option. Includes the specific literature on shadow automation and resistance behaviors in populations displaced from physical labor.

Measurement Science and Organizational Metrics — the theory and practice of designing measurement systems that shape organizational behavior, with particular attention to the documented failure modes of productivity-centric metrics in safety-critical environments, Goodhart's Law dynamics in isolated populations, and the methodology for designing passive telemetry-based composite indices that capture what matters rather than what is easy to self-report.

Phase 2 — Analog Deployment (2 years, operational)

The practitioner is embedded in isolated, confined environment operations where human-AI labor division is already in practice: Antarctic research stations with automated monitoring systems, deep-sea installations with AI-managed life support, submarine deployments with AI-assisted navigation and systems management.

In each deployment, the practitioner does not observe the workforce. They design elements of it: specifying decision topologies for the systems in use, establishing competency floor protocols for critical systems, deploying API shadow models to audit irreducibility in operational roles, measuring colony health analogs across their three components, and living inside the resulting design as a crew member for extended periods.

The critical experience that no classroom provides: witnessing the specific moment when a highly competent operator, faced with manual control of a system they have supervised AI managing for months, discovers their hands are slower than they should be. The design imperative of the Competency Floor Protocol — and the hardware air-gap that enforces it — is not fully understood until it has been witnessed in a real operational environment under real operational pressure.

Phase 3 — Lunar-Specific Integration (1 year, simulation)

Lunar-specific variables alter several workforce design parameters that Earth analogs do not replicate. Communication latency to Earth — 1.3 seconds in the near term, eventually 3 to 20 minutes for Mars operations — eliminates the option of Earth-based authority for any time-critical decision. Every decision class that might currently be escalated to Earth mission control must be classified Level 3 or Level 4 in the colony's Decision Topology, and the colonists must be designed and prepared to carry that authority competently. The lunar workforce holds more sovereign decision authority than any Earth-based crew in the history of human operations. The Latency-Bounded Safe-State Invariant must be trained under simulated latency conditions until the trigger cadence becomes operationally automatic.

Additionally, the physical demands of the lunar environment — EVA requirement for any outdoor operation, the cognitive overhead of suit-dependent physical activity, the absence of natural light cycles and their effects on circadian rhythm and cognitive performance — all modify workforce design parameters in ways that simulation training must make concrete before any practitioner contributes to a real lunar workforce specification.

Phase 3 training includes explicit psychological preparation for phase transition grief — the identity migration from physical to analytical labor. Colonists are trained to recognize the onset of shadow automation impulses in themselves and their crewmates, and to redirect those impulses into sanctioned legacy function channels.

The Phase 3 deliverable is a complete workforce design specification for a specific lunar mission scenario: Decision Topology Specification (with Safe-State Invariants per decision class), Competency Floor Registry (with hardware air-gap specifications), Colony Health Index design (with passive telemetry architecture), API configuration (with phase-dependent parameters), and Adaptive Role Architecture (with shadow automation countermeasures) for a defined roster operating across a defined mission timeline. This document is the practitioner's capstone and their primary professional credential.

